 Event Loop - 
 JavaScript is a single-threaded language, meaning it executes one operation at a time within a single execution thread (Call Stack). However, JavaScript handles asynchronous operations efficiently using the Event Loop, which manages different queues such as the Microtask Queue and the Callback Queue.

Execution Phases in JavaScript ------ 
JavaScript executes code in two major phases:

1. Synchronous Execution (Main Thread / Call Stack)
2. Asynchronous Execution (Microtask & Callback Queues)


1. Synchronous Execution (Main Thread / Call Stack)
Synchronous code is executed line-by-line from top to bottom. It gets executed immediately in the Call Stack.

How the Call Stack Works ---- 
JavaScript uses a Call Stack, which follows the LIFO (Last In, First Out) principle.
When a function is called, it is pushed onto the Call Stack.
When a function finishes execution, it is popped off the Call Stack.

Example ---- 
console.log("Step 1"); // Sync → Executed immediately
console.log("Step 2"); // Sync → Executed immediately

Execution Flow
console.log("Step 1") → Executed and removed from the Call Stack.
console.log("Step 2") → Executed and removed from the Call Stack.

Call Stack Flow

Call Stack:
-----------------
console.log("Step 1")  // Executes and removed
console.log("Step 2")  // Executes and removed
-----------------
📌 Key Point: Synchronous code runs first before any asynchronous operations.

2. Asynchronous Execution (Microtask & Callback Queues)
JavaScript can handle asynchronous operations using the Event Loop, which manages two important queues:

(1) Microtask Queue (Highest Priority)

Includes Promises (.then(), .catch(), .finally()) and queueMicrotask().
Executes immediately after synchronous code but before the Callback Queue.

(2) Callback Queue (Macrotask Queue)

Includes setTimeout, setInterval, setImmediate (Node.js), and I/O callbacks.
Executes after the Microtask Queue.


Understanding the Event Loop
The Event Loop is responsible for:

Executing all synchronous code first.
Executing all Microtasks (Promises, queueMicrotask).
Executing all Macrotasks (setTimeout, setInterval, setImmediate, etc.).


Example to Demonstrate Synchronous & Asynchronous Execution
console.log("Start");

setTimeout(() => {
  console.log("Timeout Callback");
}, 0);

Promise.resolve().then(() => {
  console.log("Promise Resolved");
});

console.log("End");


Step-by-Step Execution:
(1) Synchronous Code (Executed First)

"Start" is logged immediately.
setTimeout(...) is registered and moved to the Callback Queue (not executed yet).
Promise.resolve().then(...) is moved to the Microtask Queue (not executed yet).
"End" is logged immediately.

(2) Microtask Queue Execution (Higher Priority)

"Promise Resolved" is logged before setTimeout because Microtasks execute before Macrotasks.

(3) Callback Queue Execution (Lower Priority)

"Timeout Callback" is logged last because it is in the Callback Queue.

